\section{Rename an Imported Palette Entry Without Retargeting Charges}

In a composed workbench, renaming a palette entry can change how names resolve even in documents that do not use that entry. Consider an illustrative editor with module-qualified palette references and stable definition identifiers. It receives a resolved workspace, a target palette definition, and a proposed new name. Assume all references are initially resolved and that the editor can stage changes to all affected documents. The rename should change their text where necessary, preserve every reference's original definition identifier, and publish no edits if that cannot be achieved. The final resolution check includes references that were not explicitly edited.

\begin{verbatim}
rename_entry(workspace, target, name):
  before = resolved_links(workspace)
  draft = copy_for_edit(workspace)
  if duplicate_in_owner(draft, target, name): return Duplicate
  set_definition_name(draft, target, name)
  for (ref, def) in before:
    # Update users by identity rather than by spelling.
    if def == target: spell(ref in draft, name)
  for (ref, def) in before:
    # Qualify even unedited uses whose resolution changed.
    if resolve(draft, ref) != def: qualify(draft, ref, def)
  if resolved_links(draft) != before: return CannotRename
  return publish(draft)
\end{verbatim}

Suppose module \texttt{Court} exports \texttt{Azure} with identifier \(12\), while \texttt{Studio} exports \texttt{Blue} with identifier \(27\). In a document importing both, two charges initially use unqualified \texttt{Azure} and \texttt{Blue}. Renaming \(12\) to \texttt{Blue} leaves two same-spelled candidates for each unqualified use. The editor returns a workspace with \texttt{Court.Blue} and \texttt{Studio.Blue} at the respective charge sites; the links remain \(12,27\). If \texttt{Court} itself already defines another entry called \texttt{Blue}, the result is \texttt{Duplicate}, with no textual edits.

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-186} tracks the same definition identities through the two spelling changes.

